In France, voting is traditionally a low-tech experience: Voters isolate themselves in a cabin and put a pre-printed piece of pair of paper on which the candidate of their choice is entered into an envelope.
After officials have confirmed the identity of the electorate, the voter throws the envelope into the ballot box and signs the electoral list.
The French electoral accord defines the procedure quite strictly.
Since 1988, ballot boxes have had to be transparent in order for voters and observers to be able to testify that there are no envelopes at the beginning of the election and that no envelopes are thrown in except those of the duly counted and authorized voters.
Candidates can send representatives who attend any part of the process as witnesses. In the evening, the votes of volunteers are counted under strict supervision and according to special procedures.
