If a small group of organisms (a small population) is separated from the main population from which it originates (e.g., if they move over a mountain range or a river, or if they relocate to a new island so that they can’t easily return), they will often find themselves in a different environment than before.
This new environment has different resources and other competitors, so the new population needs different features or adjustments to be a strong competitor than what it needed before.
The original population hasn't changed at all, it still needs the same adjustments as before.
Over time, the new population begins to adapt to its new environment, and then it looks less and less similar to the other population.
After thousands or even millions of years, two populations look so different that they can no longer be assigned to the same species.
We call this procedure speciation, by which the emergence of new species is meant. Speciation is an inevitable consequence and a very important part of evolution.
