In order to understand the Templars, one needs to understand the context that led to the foundation of the Order.
The epoch in which the events took place is commonly referred to as High Middle Ages, an age in European history stretching beyond the 11th, 12th and 13th centuries (1000–1300 A.D.).
The Early Middle Ages preceded early Middle Ages, followed by the Late Middle Ages, which ends in agreement around 1500.
Technological determinism is a term encompassing a broad spectrum of ideas in practice, from the technology push or the technological imperative to the strict sense that human destiny is determined by an underlying logic associated with scientific laws and their manifestation in technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism share two general notions. On the one hand, that the development of the technology itself follows a path that is largely beyond cultural or political influence, and, on the other hand, that technology in turn has “implications” on societies that are inherent rather than socially conditioned.
It can be said, for example, that the passenger car inevitably entails the development of roads.
However, a nationwide road network for only a small number of cars is economically unsurvivable. Therefore, new production methods are being developed to reduce the cost of car ownership.
The widespread automotive possession also leads to a higher number of accidents on the roads, which in turn leads to the invention of new techniques in the healthcare sector for the repair of damaged bodies.
Romanticism had a strong, culturally deterministic element, drawn from writers such as Goethe, Spruce and Schlegel.
