Its status decreased over the course of the twelfth century, which was mainly due to the fact that the crusaders had returned with gifts such as silk and spices, which were deemed more valuable than the offer of the Byzantine markets.
At that time, the title of the fashion capital of the world also migrated from Constantinople to Paris.
