Substantive theories focus on finding what moves or appeals to people.
These theories suggest that people have certain needs and/or desires that are internalized in the course of their adulthood.
These theories deal with what causes certain people to want exactly the things they do, and what influences in their environment make them do certain things or not do.
Two popular content theories are Maslow's theory of demand hierarchy and Hertzberg's two-factor theory.
In general, two behaviors can emerge when managers begin to lead their former counterparts. The one end of the spectrum is an attempt to remain “one of the boys” (or one of the girls).
This type of manager has trouble making unpopular decisions, carrying out disciplinary action, making performance assessments, assigning responsibilities, and putting employees on duty.
At the other end of the spectrum, you turn into an unrecognisable individual who has to do everything differently than the team has done it and embrace everything.
Ultimately, the team leader is responsible for the team’s success and failure.
This behavior often leads to quarrels between the leaders and the rest of the team.
Virtual teams are led according to the same standards of excellence as conventional teams, but there are subtle differences.
Virtual team members often have the function of a touchpoint for their immediate physical group.
They often have more autonomy than traditional team members because their teams can meet in different time zones that may not be taken into account by the local leadership.
The presence of a real “invisible team” (Larson and LaFasto 1989, pp. 109) is also a unique part of a virtual team.
"The Invisible Team"" is the leadership team to which each of the members reports. The invisible team sets the standards for each member.x
Why would an organization take up the time-consuming process of introducing a learning organization? One goal for the practical implementation of organizational learning concepts is innovation.
If all available resources are used effectively through all specialist departments of an organization, creativity and ingenuity can emerge.
As a result, an organization’s collaborative work on overcoming an obstacle can lead to a new innovative process of responding to the customer’s need.
Before an organization can innovate, leadership needs to develop a culture of innovation as well as shared knowledge and organizational learning.
Angel (2006) explains the continuum concept as a method to help organizations become more powerful.
Neurobiological data provides physical evidence for a theoretical approach to the study of cognition. Thus, they limit the field of research and make it much more accurate.
The correlation between brain pathology and behavior supports scientists in their research.
It has long been known that different types of brain damage, such as traumas, injuries and tumors, influence the behavior and lead to changes in some brain functions.
Newly emerging technologies enable us to see and investigate certain brain structures and processes for the first time.
This provides us with a lot of information and material to build simulation models that help us understand the processes in our mind.
AI is strongly associated with science fiction, but is an important branch of computer science that engages in behavior, learning, and intelligent machine customization.
Research in the field of AI includes the production of machines for the automation of tasks that require intelligent behavior.
Examples include control, creating strategies and schedules, the ability to answer customer diagnoses and questions, as well as handwriting, voice and facial recognition.
Such things have become independent disciplines focused on finding solutions to real-life problems.
Today, the AI system is often used in economics, medicine, engineering and the military and has also been integrated into numerous software applications for home computers and video games.
Excursions are an important component for every school class. Not infrequently, a teacher would like to take her students to places where a bus ride is out of the question.
Virtual excursions represent a technical solution. Students can look at museum pieces, visit an aquarium or admire beautiful works of art from the classroom.
Sharing an excursion virtually is also a great way to reflect on a journey and share experiences with future classes.
For example, students at Bennet School in North Carolina design a website every year about their trip to the state’s capital. The website is remodeled every year, but the previous versions remain online and serve as a collectible.
Blogs can also contribute to improving students’ writing skills, although students often have sloppy grammar and spelling at the beginning of their blog experience, that usually changes when there’s an audience.
Since students are often a very critical audience, the blog author struggles to improve his writing style to avoid criticism.
Blogging “forces students to learn more about the world around them.” The need to pique the interest of the audience inspires students to write wisely and interestingly (Toto, 2004).
Blogging is a tool that inspires collaboration and encourages students to continue learning far outside the traditional school day.
The appropriate use of blogs “can enable pupils to become more analytical and critical; through the active response to internet materials, students can define their positions in the context of the texts of others and present their own perspectives on specific topics” (Oravec, 2002).
Ottawa is Canada’s enchanting bilingual capital and features a range of art galleries and museums that showcase Canada’s past and present.
Further to the south are Niagara Falls, and to the northread the unspoiled beauty of nature spreads in Muskoka and beyond.
All of these things and more characterize Ontario as what is considered by outsiders to be typically Canadian.
Large areas further north are quite thinly populated and some are almost uninhabited wilderness.
A population comparison that surprises many: There are more African-Americans living in the U.S. than Canada has citizens.
The East African islands are located in the Indian Ocean off the east coast of Africa.
Madagascar is by far the largest and, in terms of wildlife, its own continent.
Most of the smaller islands are independent nations or belonging to France and are known as luxurious seaside resorts.
The Arabs also brought Islam into the country, and it was very successful in Comoros and Mayotte.
European influence and colonialism began in the 15th century, when Portuguese navigator Vasco da Gama spotted the cape route from Europe to India.
To the north, the region is bounded by the Sahel zone and to the south and west by the Atlantic Ocean.
