Remain consistent when rejecting men, and don’t shy away from defending your point of view (cultural differences or not; that doesn’t mean it’s okay yet!)
Today's Casablanca was founded by Berber fishermen in the 10th century before the beginning of the era and was used by the Phoenicians, the Romans and Merinids as a strategic port called Anfa.
The Portuguese destroyed it and rebuilt it under the name Casa Branca, only to abandon it after an earthquake in 1755.
The Moroccan sultan rebuilt the city as Dar el Beida and it was called Casablanca by Spanish merchants who established trade bases there.
Casablanca is one of the most uninteresting places to shop throughout Morocco.
Around the old medina it is easy to find places where traditional Moroccan goods such as tagines, ceramics, leather goods, hookahs and a whole spectrum of tinnef are sold, but that's all just for the tourists.
Goma is a tourist city of the Democratic Republic of Congo in the far east of Rwanda.
In 2002, Goma was destroyed by the lava of the Nyiragongo volcano, which buried most of the city’s streets, especially the city center.
While Goma is reasonably safe, every stay outside Gomas should be carefully planned to learn about the status of the fighting continued in North Kivu province.
The city is also the starting point for ascending Nyiragongo Volcano, as well as some of the cheapest mountain gorilla tracking tours in Africa.
You can use Bodabodas (motorbike taxis) to get around Goma. The usual (local) price is at ~500 Congolese francs for a short drive.x
This in combination with its relative inaccessibility has made “Timbuktu” a metaphor for exotic, distant lands.
Although Timbuktu is considered a tourist attraction due to its reputation and owns an airport, nowadays it is an impoverished city.
In 1990 it was added to the list of endangered world heritage areas due to the threat posed by desert sand.
It was one of Louis Gate’s PBS special “Wonders of the African World”.
The city stands in stark contrast to the rest of the country's cities because it has an Arab rather than African flair.
Kruger National Park (KNP) is located in the north-east of South Africa, bordering Mozambique to the east, Zimbabwe to the north and the Crocodile River to the south.
The park has an area of 19,500 km2 and is divided into 14 different eco-zones, each of which supports a different wildlife.
It is one of South Africa’s main attractions and is considered the figurehead of South African National Parks (SANParks).
Like all South African national parks, the park charges daily protection and entrance fees.
It may also be worthwhile to buy a wild card that grants access either to selected parks in South Africa or to all South African national parks.
Hong Kong Island is the namesake for the territory of Hong Kong and is the focus for many tourists.
The buildings that make up the Hong Kong skyline were compared to a dazzling bar chart visible through the presidence of Victoria Harbour's waters.
For the best views of Hong Kong you should leave the island and drive to the opposite shore of Kowloon.
The bulk of Hong Kong Island's urbanization is densely crowded on newly recovered land along the north coast.
This is the place that the British colonists have embraced. If you’re looking for traces of the colonial past of the area, that’s a good starting point.
The Sundarbans are the largest mangrove belt in the world and extend over 80 km from the Bangladese and Indian hinterlands to the coast.
The Sundarbans have been declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The part of the forest, which lies within India's national territory, is referred to as Sundarbans National Park.
However, the forests aren’t just mangrove swamps – they also include some of the last remaining stocks of the mighty jungle that once covered the ganges plain.
Sundarbans cover an area of 3,850 km2, of which one-third is covered by water and marshes.
The Sundarbans have been a nature reserve since 1966, and it is estimated that there are now 400 Bengalic royal tigers and about 30,000 spotted deer.
Buses depart all day from cross-district bus station (on the other side of the river), with most buses, especially those heading east and Jakar/Bumthang, depart between 06:30 a.m. and 7:30 a.m.
Since the buses between the districts are often full, it is advisable to buy a ticket a few days in advance.
Most districts are driven by small Japanese coaster buses that are cozy and stable.x
Collective taxis provide a quick and convenient way to get to nearby places such as Paro (Nu 150) and Punakha (Nu 200).
The Oyapock River Bridge is a cable-stayed bridge that spans the Oyapock River and connects the cities of Oiapoque in Brazil and Saint-Georges de l'Oyapock in French Guiana.
Its two towers are 83 meters high, its length is 378 meters and it has two lanes with a width of 3.50 m.
The passage height under the bridge is 15 meters. The construction work was completed in August 2011, but the release for the traffic took place only in March 2017.
The bridge is due to start operations in full in September 2017. It is expected that Brazilian customs checkpoints will then be completed.
The Guaraní were the most important indigenous group that inhabited present-day eastern Paraguay. They lived as semi-nomadic hunters, who also pursued subsistence agriculture.
The Chaco region was also home to other groups of native tribes, such as Guaycurú and Payaguá, who survived by hunting, collecting and fishing.
Paraguay, formerly known as West Indie Province, emerged in the 16th century as a result of the convergence of Spanish conquerors with the native indigenous groups.
The Spaniards began the colonization period, which lasted for three centuries.
Since the founding of Asunción in 1537, Paraguay has managed to preserve much of its indigenous character and identity.
Argentina is known to be among the best in the world for its polo team and players.
The biggest tournament of the year takes place in December on Las Cañita's polo fields.
Smaller tournaments and competitions can also be watched here at other times of the year.x
You can find information on competitions and outlets for polo game tickets at Asociacion Argentina de Polo.
The official currency of the Falkland Islands is the Falkland Pound (FKP), whose value is determined in accordance with the value of the British Pound (GBP).
Money can be exchanged in the only bank on the island, which is located in Stanley opposite the FIC West Store.
The British pound is generally accepted everywhere on the islands and with Stanley credit cards. U.S. dollars are also frequently accepted.
On secluded islands, credit cards are unlikely to be accepted, but UK and U.S. currency can be accepted; inquire with the owners in advance which payment method will be accepted.
It is almost impossible to exchange the currency of the Falkland Islands outside the islands itself, so you should exchange the money from the island prior to departure.
Since Montevideo is located south of the equator, it is summer there when it is winter in the northern hemisphere and vice versa.
Montevideo is located in the subtropics. Temperatures above +30 °C are common in the summer months.
Winter can be deceptively chilly: Temperatures seldom go below freezing, but wind and moisture make it feel colder than what the thermometer indicates.
There are no pronounced rainy or dry seasons: the amount of rain remains roughly the same throughout the year.
Although many of the animals in the park see the sight of humans, the wildlife is still wild and should not be fed or disturbed.
According to instructions from the park authority, visitors should stay at least 100 yard/meter away from bears and wolves and 25 yard/meters from all other wild animals!
No matter how peaceful bison, wapitis, moose, bears and practically all large animals can work, it can happen that they turn to attack.
Dozens of visitors are injured every year because they don't keep enough distance. These animals are large, wild, and potentially dangerous, so leave them plenty of space.
Also, be aware that odors can attract bears and other wildlife, so avoid carrying or cooking heavily fragrant food, and pay attention to a clean warehouse.
Apia is the capital of Samoa, located on Upolu Island and has a population of just under 40,000.
Apia was founded in the '50s and has been the official capital of Samoa since 1959.
The port was the scene of an inglorious stalemate of the navy in 1889, when seven ships from Germany, the United States and Britain refused to leave the port.
All ships with the exception of a British cruiser were sunk. Nearly 200 Americans and Germans died.
During the struggle for independence organized by the Mau movement, a peaceful gathering in the city led to the killing of the supreme chief Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III.
Since Auckland stretches across two ports, there are many beaches. The most popular beaches are located in three areas.
The beaches of North Shore (in North Harbour district) are located on the Pacific Ocean and extend from Long Bay in the north to Devonport to the south.
Almost all are sandy beaches with safe bathing facilities and most offer shade donated by Pohutukawa trees.
Tamaki Drive's beaches are located at Waitemata Harbour, in the upscaled Mission Bay suburbs, as well as St Heliers in Central Auckland.
There are family beaches, which are sometimes crowded, with a beautiful shopping promenade along the coast. Swimming is safe here.
The maining local beer is “Number One”. It’s not complex beer, but pleasant and refreshing. The other local beer is called “Manta”.
There are plenty of French wines to drink, but New Zealand and Australian wines may be better tolerated.
The local tap water can be drunk without hesitation, but if you are worried, you will also easily find bottled water.
