The capital of Moldova is Kischinau. The native language is Romanian, but many people also speak Russian.
Moldova is a multi-ethnic republic that has suffered from ethnic conflicts.
In 1994, this conflict led to the establishment of the self-proclaimed Transnistrian Republic in eastern Moldova, which has its own government and currency, but is not recognized by any U.N. member state.
Despite the failure of the political negotiations, economic relations between these two parts of Moldova could be restored.
The most important religion in Moldova is Christian Orthodox.
Izmir is Turkey's third-largest city with a population of about 3.7 million, the second-largest port after Istanbul and a very good traffic hub.
The former ancient city of Smyrna is now a modern, progressive and bustling business center located on a huge bay surrounded by mountains.
The broad boulevards, glazed buildings and modern shopping malls are characterized by classic red tile roofs, 18th-century market, as well as old mosques and churches, although the city exudes an atmosphere of Mediterranean Europe rather than that of traditional Turkey.
